// Anonymous class: ~5 lines to express one comparison
Comparator<String> byLen = new Comparator<String>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(String a, String b) {
        return Integer.compare(a.length(), b.length());
    }
};
// here 'this' refers to the anonymous Comparator, not the outer class
```

```java
// Lambda: the compiler infers the Comparator<String> target type
Comparator<String> byLen =
    (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a.length(), b.length());

// in an instance context, 'this' keeps its enclosing meaning
```

*Reach for an anonymous class when you need a class body with its own state or helpers, a distinct `this`, or implementations for a multi-method contract.*

Here is the same total computed both ways, so the contrast is concrete:

```java
import java.util.List;

public class StreamVsLoop {
    record Order(String item, double price) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var orders = List.of(new Order("Pen", 5), new Order("Bag", 80),
                             new Order("Cup", 12));

        // for-loop: explicit; pick it when you need an index, break, or mutation
        double total = 0;
        for (Order o : orders) {
            if (o.price() > 10) total += o.price();
        }

        // stream: declarative pipeline that reads like the intent
        double streamTotal = orders.stream()
            .filter(o -> o.price() > 10)
            .map(Order::price)
            .reduce(0.0, Double::sum);

        System.out.println(total + " == " + streamTotal); // 92.0 == 92.0
    }
}
```

The loop is explicit and easy to single-step in a debugger, while the stream reads as filter, then map, then sum. Both print the same number, so the real choice is clarity versus low-level control, not correctness.

