This pairs a default method on an interface with a stateful abstract class, so the split is visible side by side:

```java
public class DefaultVsAbstract {
    // Interface + default method: shared behavior, no state, multiple inheritance
    interface Greeter {
        String name();
        default String greet() { return "Hi, " + name(); } // default body
    }

    // Abstract class: shared behavior + instance state + constructor
    abstract static class Account {
        protected double balance;                 // instance state
        Account(double opening) { balance = opening; }
        void deposit(double a) { balance += a; }
        abstract String type();                   // subclass contract
    }

    record Person(String name) implements Greeter {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Person("Sam").greet()); // Hi, Sam
    }
}
```

Greeter shares behavior with no state, so a record can implement it freely. Account needs a balance field and a constructor to seed it, which only an abstract class can provide.

Here several Java 8 features cooperate in one small, compilable example:

```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class OrderProcessor {
    enum Status { PENDING, COMPLETED }

    static final class Order {
        private final long id;
        private final long customerId;
        private final double total;
        private final Status status;

        Order(long id, long customerId, double total, Status status) {
            this.id = id;
            this.customerId = customerId;
            this.total = total;
            this.status = status;
        }

        long getId() { return id; }
        long getCustomerId() { return customerId; }
        double getTotal() { return total; }
        Status getStatus() { return status; }
    }

    interface OrderRepository {
        List<Order> findByCustomerId(long customerId);
        Optional<Order> findById(long orderId);
    }

    private final OrderRepository repository;

    OrderProcessor(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    Optional<Order> findHighestCompletedOrder(long customerId) {
        return repository.findByCustomerId(customerId).stream()
            .filter(order -> order.getStatus() == Status.COMPLETED)
            .max(Comparator.comparingDouble(Order::getTotal))
            .map(this::addLoyaltyBonus);
    }

    void processOrder(long orderId) {
        repository.findById(orderId)
            .filter(order -> order.getTotal() > 100)
            .ifPresent(this::sendConfirmationEmail);
    }

    private Order addLoyaltyBonus(Order order) {
        return new Order(order.getId(), order.getCustomerId(),
            order.getTotal() * 1.1, order.getStatus());
    }

    private void sendConfirmationEmail(Order order) {
        System.out.println("Confirmed order " + order.getId());
    }
}
```

The stream uses a lambda for filtering and a method reference for comparison. Its `max` result is an Optional, whose `map` runs only when an order exists. The second method uses an Optional filter and a bound method reference.
